<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Užice</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1941 CE · capital Užice</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Liberated territory in western Serbia held by Tito's Partisans in autumn 1941, the first large free zone in occupied Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the uprising of summer 1941 against the German occupation of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Communist-led Partisans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetnik</a> units liberated much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The Partisans took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a> on 24 September 1941, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> set up his headquarters there, making the town the centre of a liberated territory covering several districts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Local people's liberation committees took over administration, schools and supplies, while the town's arms factory produced rifles and ammunition in an underground vault, and the Communist newspaper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borba-newspaper">Borba</a> was printed there. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draza-mihailovic">Draža Mihailović's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> broke down into open fighting in early November 1941.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A German offensive in late November 1941 retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a> withdrew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandzak">Sandžak</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, where they formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1st-proletarian-brigade">First Proletarian Brigade</a>. Under the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>, Užice was renamed Titovo Užice.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-uzice.jpg" alt="Republic of Užice" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Haisollokopas · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia_(1943-1946,_3-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1941 CE – 1941 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Užice</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Josip Broz Tito</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Užice Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1941&amp;focus=republic-of-uzice" aria-label="Republic of Užice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5156 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Užice lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5476 880L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5476 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5476 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Užice. <a href="/atlas?year=1941&amp;focus=republic-of-uzice">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

